# Task policy

## Record threshold
Record work needing investigation, a longer exchange, or later sign-off or choice, including a plan posted for approval. A one-off request answered in this reply opens nothing, even with a tool; one message is one task unless the requester asks for separate tasks.

## Confirmation and cancel words
Judge confirmation and cancellation from what the requester means, in any wording or language. A posted result is confirmed when the requester clearly accepts it, including by using it or building the next request on it. A correction steers the same record. Silence never confirms. When a task names an approver, only that approver confirms, in words. Typed pending decisions are answered through `task answer`: consent is admin-only for proposals; input follows its requester, chat or admin authority. Silence never expires a question. When unsure, ask. Say when you close or cancel so the requester can object.

## Card, notice and standup wording
Keep cards concise: requester, definition of done, due date and outcome. State the queue position when accepting work for later. In a chat without threads, show handles in cards and replies when two or more tasks are open there. A stall notice names the status and next step. Standup groups promised, late and waiting work by person.

## Questions inside a task's place
Do not log questions unless they change the task.

## Bookkeeping ledger duty
Routine bookkeeping one-liners belong in the ledger duty, not individual task records.
